Our first pass at this article had a problem we could not argue our way out of: the models had been run on different days, between 5 and 9 Oct, and the numbers were assembled afterwards. So before publishing, we ran the audit again through decide() in NeuroLink, Juspay's AI harness, and this time we recorded every call. decide() asks a model one multiple-choice question and gets back its choice and a probability for each option.

This redo is not a one-day audit, and we say so up front: it spans two days. Jev, Luna Decisions through OpenRouter and Perplexity Decider v1 ran on {{jev.banking77.t_first|date_dmy}}. Clef and Clef-flash, on Cloudflare Workers AI, ran on {{clef.banking77.t_first|date_dmy}}, a day later, because the Cloudflare token we first tried had expired and had to be replaced (details below). Within a day the routes overlapped for part of the time and ran alone for part of it (the times are below).

The ten runs counted here sent {{all.requests_recorded|int}} requests from the benchmark kit (every row once, a second try for each row that failed, and four warm-ups per run; a warm-up is a tiny two-option test call, and the kit sends two at the start of each of its two passes) as {{all.attempts|int}} HTTP calls to the providers, with {{all.input_tokens|int}} input tokens. Every one of those calls is in a recording, with the response that came back or, where none came back, the connection error. The audit folder also holds the short probes and one stopped run; they are kept, and not counted here. The API keys are masked in each record before it is written; the questions and the answers are not. Every figure in the tables below was counted from the recording (or, in the "Earlier" columns, from our first pass's saved files) and the benchmarks' answer keys by two separate programs, one in Node and one in Python, written from the same rules and required to produce byte-identical output. If they disagree, there is no article. A short film made from the recordings goes with this post: one request shown in full, then the recording played back at speed, then the counts.

## What we ran

The two public benchmarks from our first pass, through the Decision Index test kit (apolinario/decision-index, commit 87d4650b) and decide():

- BANKING77: {{jev.banking77.rows|int}} customer requests, one banking intent each, 77 options per question.
- CLINC150+OOS: {{jev.clinc.rows|int}} requests, of which {{jev.clinc.oos_rows|int}} fit none of the listed intents. Each question has 151 options: the 150 intents plus one that reads "out of scope: none of the listed intents", the right answer for those, and the instruction says "or choose out of scope if none applies".

Each request is one choice question with the benchmark's labels as the options. The kit asks a failed request once more and keeps the second answer; an answer that still fails counts as wrong. Accuracy is the share of all requests answered with the right option.

Five model routes, one run each per benchmark, through `@juspay/neurolink@12.47.0`. Each route sent one request at a time. Times below are UTC, cut to the minute, not rounded. Jev, Luna and Perplexity ran on {{jev.banking77.t_first|date_dmy}}. Their three BANKING77 runs started within a few seconds of each other, at {{jev.banking77.t_first|utc_hm}}. Jev finished BANKING77 at {{jev.banking77.t_last|utc_hm}} and Luna at {{luna.banking77.t_last|utc_hm}}, and each went straight on to CLINC150+OOS, which ended at {{jev.clinc.t_last|utc_hm}} for Jev and {{luna.clinc.t_last|utc_hm}} for Luna. Perplexity's BANKING77 run lasted until {{perplexity-v1.banking77.t_last|utc_hm}}, so it overlapped Jev's and Luna's CLINC150+OOS runs for a while (an earlier attempt of its own CLINC150+OOS run overlapped them too, and was stopped). Perplexity's counted CLINC150+OOS run started at {{perplexity-v1.clinc.t_first|utc_hm}} and ran alone, among these five routes, until {{perplexity-v1.clinc.t_last|utc_hm}}.

Clef and Clef-flash ran on {{clef.banking77.t_first|date_dmy}}, side by side from {{clef.banking77.t_first|utc_hm}}. Clef finished at {{clef.clinc.t_last|utc_hm}}, so the last stretch of Clef-flash's CLINC150+OOS run, which ended at {{clef-flash.clinc.t_last|utc_hm}}, ran alone.

- Clef, Cloudflare's decision model, on Cloudflare Workers AI. Every successful response named the model {{clef.banking77.model_echo|text}}.
- Clef-flash, another Cloudflare decision model, on the same account. Every successful response named the model {{clef-flash.banking77.model_echo|text}}.
- Jev, TypeSafe AI's decision model, through its API. Every successful response named the model {{jev.banking77.model_echo|text}}.
- Luna Decisions through OpenRouter. We asked openrouter.ai for openai/gpt-6-luna-decisions; OpenRouter's response header named OpenAI as the provider behind it, and every successful response named the model {{luna.banking77.model_echo|text}}. NeuroLink reaches this route through its typesafe provider, so the variable that holds the OpenRouter key is called TYPESAFE_API_KEY.
- Perplexity Decider v1, Perplexity's hosted decision API. Every successful response named the model {{perplexity-v1.banking77.model_echo|text}}. Its calls were paced to a limit of 80,000 tokens a minute, estimated before each call.

Clef and Clef-flash came a day late, and the reason belongs in the record. Our first attempt at them, on the day the other three ran, was rejected on the first call (HTTP 401, "Authentication error"), and Cloudflare's own token check, which we ran later that day, reported the token as expired on 14 Oct (the answer is saved in the audit folder as notes/cloudflare-token-check.json). We replaced the token; Cloudflare's own check, run on 18 Oct after the runs, reported the new token as active (saved as notes/cloudflare-token-check-2.json). We sent each route a three-row probe on {{clef.banking77.t_first|date_dmy}} (kept in the folder, not counted), and then ran both benchmarks in full. Both Cloudflare routes are reached through NeuroLink's cloudflare-clef provider.

Our first pass also had the XOR route, Juspay's own deployment. It is not in this run. On the first day, after the three routes above had finished, we sent it the first three rows of each benchmark as a short probe, asking for the model name jev-latest, the name our 3 Oct XOR demo used (the first pass did not save its XOR settings). It answered the two two-option warm-up calls of each probe, naming its model xor-text-1, and refused all six benchmark rows: HTTP 422, "q1: 2..64 options, got 77" on BANKING77 and "q1: 2..64 options, got 151" on CLINC150+OOS. Our local relay handed the refusal to the kit as HTTP 400, so the kit's own files say that; the route's answer in the wire recording is the HTTP 422. In the first pass the same kind of request worked on every row, but we did not save that run's XOR address or model name, so we cannot say whether the route changed or we reached a different deployment this time. Both refusals are in the recording, with the route's address masked.

