Clef-flash did not repeat itself. On BANKING77 its accuracy moved {{clef-flash.banking77.vs_blog.accuracy|pp1}} ({{clef-flash.banking77.vs_blog.correct|signed}} requests). On CLINC150+OOS it moved {{clef-flash.clinc.vs_blog.accuracy|pp1}} ({{clef-flash.clinc.vs_blog.correct|signed}} requests) while it became much less willing to say "out of scope": it caught {{clef-flash.clinc.oos_caught|int}} of the {{clef-flash.clinc.oos_rows|int}} such requests against {{blog.clef-flash.clinc.oos_caught|int}} earlier, but gave that answer {{clef-flash.clinc.said_oos|int}} times in all against {{blog.clef-flash.clinc.said_oos|int}} earlier, so more of its "out of scope" answers were right ({{clef-flash.clinc.precision|pct1}} against {{blog.clef-flash.clinc.precision|pct1}}). Both runs asked for the model name clef-flash, but the first pass's saved files hold no responses, so we cannot compare the model name the API answered with, or say whether the model behind that name changed.

Perplexity Decider v1 moved too. On BANKING77 its accuracy moved {{perplexity-v1.banking77.vs_blog.accuracy|pp1}} ({{perplexity-v1.banking77.vs_blog.correct|signed}} requests). On CLINC150+OOS it moved {{perplexity-v1.clinc.vs_blog.accuracy|pp1}} ({{perplexity-v1.clinc.vs_blog.correct|signed}} requests) while it became much less willing to say "out of scope": it caught {{perplexity-v1.clinc.oos_caught|int}} of the {{perplexity-v1.clinc.oos_rows|int}} such requests against {{blog.perplexity-v1.clinc.oos_caught|int}} earlier, and more of its "out of scope" answers were right ({{perplexity-v1.clinc.precision|pct1}} against {{blog.perplexity-v1.clinc.precision|pct1}}). Both Perplexity runs asked for pplx-decider-v1-27b, but the first pass did not record the model name the API answered with, so we cannot say whether the model behind that name changed.

One run each tells you the numbers moved, not why.

Failed answers count as wrong. Jev had {{jev.banking77.failed|int}} on BANKING77 and {{jev.clinc.failed|int}} on CLINC150+OOS ({{blog.jev.banking77.failed|int}} and {{blog.jev.clinc.failed|int}} earlier). In this run every one was a response whose probabilities summed to 0.99. The kit rejects a sum more than 0.01 away from one; floating-point rounding put some of those sums just over that line, and other responses with the same sum passed. Luna had {{luna.clinc.failed|int}} on CLINC150+OOS: one request came back HTTP 502 on all four calls (the kit's two tries, each repeated once inside NeuroLink), every time with the message “OpenAI refused to answer question "q1"”. The same request failed with HTTP 502 in our first pass too. Luna's other two repeats followed one HTTP 503 and one call that got no reply, and both succeeded. No other cell in this run has a failed answer, Clef and Clef-flash included.

