"Retried inside NeuroLink" counts the HTTP calls NeuroLink repeated after a connection error or an error reply, on top of the kit's own second try. Most of them are Perplexity's on CLINC150+OOS: {{perplexity-v1.clinc.network_errors|int}} connection errors, {{perplexity-v1.clinc.status.429|int}} rate-limit replies (HTTP 429) and {{perplexity-v1.clinc.status.504|int}} gateway timeouts (HTTP 504). NeuroLink repeated all but one of those failed calls: one request failed five calls in a row, and the kit then asked it again and it succeeded. Clef and Clef-flash needed no repeats at all: every call of their CLINC150+OOS runs was answered with HTTP 200 ({{clef.clinc.status.200|int}} of {{clef.clinc.attempts|int}} calls for Clef, {{clef-flash.clinc.status.200|int}} of {{clef-flash.clinc.attempts|int}} for Clef-flash), and the same holds for their BANKING77 runs ({{clef.banking77.status.200|int}} of {{clef.banking77.attempts|int}} and {{clef-flash.banking77.status.200|int}} of {{clef-flash.banking77.attempts|int}}).

The ten counted runs came to an estimated {{all.cost_usd|usd2}}: the input tokens the providers reported times a per-token rate, with output tokens priced at zero. For Luna the rate is OpenRouter's listed price; OpenRouter reports a cost on every successful response, and the sum of those ({{luna.banking77.cost_reported_usd|usd4}} and {{luna.clinc.cost_reported_usd|usd4}}) equals our figure. For Jev, Perplexity, Clef and Clef-flash the rates come from NeuroLink's own price table. Jev's and Perplexity's were last checked against the providers on 20 Sep and 2 Oct. Clef costs 0.24 USD per million input tokens and Clef-flash 0.09 USD. Output tokens are priced at zero: the table lists no output price for them, and Cloudflare's replies report {{clef.banking77.output_tokens|int}} output tokens. Jev also reported {{jev.banking77.output_tokens|int}} output tokens on BANKING77 and {{jev.clinc.output_tokens|int}} on CLINC150+OOS, priced at zero because the table notes that TypeSafe bills input tokens only. Perplexity reported {{perplexity-v1.banking77.output_tokens|int}} and {{perplexity-v1.clinc.output_tokens|int}}, one for each answered call, also priced at zero because the table says its rate charges nothing for output. None of it is an invoice. The median call is the time of one round trip over the calls that got a successful reply, measured while other routes were running (except on Perplexity's CLINC150+OOS run and the last stretch of Clef-flash's), so it is not a latency benchmark.

Cloudflare also meters its own work: every reply carries the neurons it used in a header, cf-ai-neurons. NeuroLink's price table says the header matched Cloudflare's published rates when it was checked on 3 Oct, at 21,818 neurons per million input tokens for Clef and 8,182 neurons per million input tokens for Clef-flash. In these runs the header adds up to {{clef.banking77.neurons_reported|neurons0}} and {{clef.clinc.neurons_reported|neurons0}} neurons for Clef, which agrees with the table's rate for Clef to within a fraction of a percent. For Clef-flash it adds up to {{clef-flash.banking77.neurons_reported|neurons0}} and {{clef-flash.clinc.neurons_reported|neurons0}} neurons, fewer than half of what the table's rate for Clef-flash gives for the same tokens, so the Clef-flash cost above and the total may be too high. We do not know why. The table keeps the list prices.

The keys are masked inside the recording itself, not only on screen: the Authorization header of every counted call shows the key as `«masked:NAME»`, NAME being the variable that held it, and Cloudflare's account number, which is part of its request address, is masked too. Cloudflare's replies also carry a cookie with a value shaped like an IP address; the recorder masks any such value as the call is recorded (IPV4_ADDRESS, {{clef.banking77.masked.IPV4_ADDRESS|int}} records in Clef's BANKING77 run). Two recordings were masked again after they were recorded, for IP addresses: two Cloudflare error pages and two connection-error messages in Perplexity's counted CLINC150+OOS run, and the headers of one XOR probe; a REMASK.json next to each lists the file's hash before and after. Before publishing, the gateway's own header values in both XOR probes and the home-folder paths in the kit's logs were replaced too; CLEANING.json in the audit folder lists those files with their hashes before and after, and that pass changed no wire or decide recording of the counted runs. Afterwards a scan looked for every value, 8 characters or longer, of a key-, token-, secret-, password-, account- or address-named variable in the environment files the runs read, as they are today, plain and encoded, in {{all.files_scanned|int}} files of the audit folder (the film and its poster image are skipped as media), and found {{all.secret_hits|int}}.

## How the numbers were made

- A recorder inside the NeuroLink process writes every attempt, masks keys before writing, allows only each route's own host, and stops a run when its booked spend reaches a cap (none did; the one stopped run stopped on failed connections).
- Two fact engines count everything from the recording, the kit's results file and the benchmarks' answer keys. The audit's facts file is written only if both produce the same bytes.
- Pointed at the saved files of our first pass, the same engines reproduce every number of its two benchmark tables exactly. That is how "Earlier" above was made, Clef and Clef-flash included. The first pass's other figures, its run-to-run table on other sets, are not recounted here, and its XOR numbers are not used in this article (the engines still recount XOR's two benchmark cells, which is why the twelve saved files include them).
- The texts are filled from the facts file, and a checker fails any number that is neither a fact nor a declared literal. The literals were checked by hand: the error codes and messages, the option counts and the kit commit against the recordings; the Node versions against each run's run.json; the token dates against the two saved token checks; the 0.01 limit against the kit's source; the name our 3 Oct XOR demo asked for against that demo's saved run files; and the first-pass dates, the price-table dates and rates, and the tokens-a-minute limit against our first-pass notes, NeuroLink's price table and the audit config. The film with this post was built the same way: every figure on screen is read back from the frames and compared with the facts.
- One command, `bash verify.sh`, re-runs the counting offline and checks every file's hash. It needs the decide-proof toolkit, Node, Python, the two benchmarks' files and the twelve saved files of our first pass. The audit folder, the toolkit, the two benchmarks' files and the twelve saved first-pass files are all public at https://github.com/juspay/neurolink-decide-proof-evidence, where `bash verify-all.sh` re-runs the check.

## What one run can and cannot show

- One run per model and benchmark: we did not measure run-to-run variation in this redo.
- Two days. Each route sent one request at a time; within a day the routes overlapped for part of the time, except Perplexity's counted CLINC150+OOS run and the last stretch of Clef-flash's, which ran alone. Clef and Clef-flash ran a day after the others, on a replaced token, so the five routes were not measured under the same conditions.
- The runs of {{jev.banking77.t_first|date_dmy}} used Node 26.11.1 and the runs of {{clef.banking77.t_first|date_dmy}} used Node 24.14.1, and the recording code was updated in between (the versions and the file hashes are in each run's run.json).
- Failed connections from this machine (host-unreachable, address-not-available and connect-timeout errors, the last ten in a row) stopped one Perplexity run; it was run again from the start, and the stopped run is kept in the folder, not counted.
- These are counts on two benchmarks, not a ranking and not a Decision Index score.

